We present Piezo-Ultrasonic Backscatter (PUB), the first ultrasonic backscatter system to achieve high-throughput, ultra-low-power underwater communication. By moving to ultrasonic frequency bands, PUB can operate over a wider bandwidth and achieve higher throughput. This shift to ultrasonics, however, requires a new node and a new physical layer design, as existing backscatter systems cannot support reliable wideband communication. PUB introduces three key innovations: First, we propose a quantized FSK (FSK-Q) modulation scheme that quadruples spectral efficiency and increases data throughput. Second, we design a new retrodirective node with cascaded switching that supports our FSK-Q scheme and maintains sufficient SNR over practical ranges. Third, we develop an efficient receiver pipeline based on a low-rank RLS algorithm, enabling robust and real-time decoding. We implemented and evaluated an end-to-end prototype of PUB in a river and a pool. In a practical river environment, PUB achieved a goodput of 13.1 kbps, a 7.1 × improvement over the state of the art at the same distance. We also integrated PUB with a wireless underwater camera; its improved throughput increased the frame rate by 41 × and reduced energy consumption by 11 × , showcasing its use in practical underwater sensing applications. Demo Video: https://youtu.be/i8wLPe9qorw